**POV.** Cael ≈30,100 (≈92.7%); Seln ≈2,350 (ch 18, scenes 1–2, ≈7.2%). Seln's motive is never stated. Nothing about loyalty or switching. The case is not mentioned. He knows only his brief, his cover and what he saw. "Colder country" is not used.

**Development beats (author's count).** Cael, 14:
1. He writes the noticed thing before judging it (15).
2. He refuses to build on an absence until he has a control (15).
3. He asks the fighter's question of a building (15).
4. He calls his own test useless aloud (15).
5. "He needs a crowd" means *working* (16).
6. He tests the aim sentence against its rivals (16).
7. He names being aimed at without flattery (16).
8. "Better at this than I am" (16).
9. He accepts being outvoted the other way: he does nothing, on purpose (17).
10. He prices the read before the day it comes due (17).
11. A hole has edges: the negative becomes a purchase (18).
12. Surveillance becomes Path theory (18).
13. He says the rest out loud (19).
14. The error is read as an exchange, not a victory (19).
15. "Not a rule till three" twice; the question about who built the instruments (21).

Supporting beats: Lira (the vote price; reads the whole column; spends exactly eleven seconds; won't throw a final; reads the log upside down); Brom (answers Lira; the corner; stops turning; pencil); Karis (tests whose; the sum; the same purse; one instrument; checks the calendar); Fiske (whole-column reader; the nod; teaches a first-year); Rooke (three words at the bench); Bracken ("feeds"/"evidence"); Ephram (corrects himself aloud); Withrow (guests); the porter (lamp paste, laces); the Current lecturer ("Ice, not heat", his first non-hour sentence); Seln. No deuteragonist.

