    50	
    51	## Internal consistency and disclosure checks
    52	
    53	### Canon
    54	
    55	No canon evidence was supplied, so there is no basis for declaring a canon violation. Names, institutions, prior events, and Path rules can only be checked for consistency within these seven chapters.
    56	
    57	### Physical state
    58	
    59	Physical consequences carry well. The cord drill strikes Cael's shin in Chapter 16; the delayed read becomes two falls and a rib bruise against Lira in Chapter 19; the lecturer advises ice. Brom's bout leaves a marked jaw, a depleted guard, and a left arm he carries “as if it belonged to somebody else,” followed by the note that his head still hurts. Lira's four bursts against Nyle are explicitly priced through the hip line and next-day cost. I found no internal physical-state contradiction.
    60	
    61	### Power limits
    62	
    63	The movement distinguishes hypotheses from rules unusually carefully. Cael labels the first late read “one instance,” tests recovery after four days off, records two instances, and refuses to call it a rule. His Log restates the Wind-adjacent limits, the unchanged landing beat, the read's range, and the five capabilities not advanced since Greyvane. The Shadow analysis progresses from “needs a crowd” to a continuous attention toll and late-day fatigue, but Cael keeps a “cannot reach” column and admits that much of it still rests on one instrument. Seln's viewpoint confirms that a real Path effect exists, while not granting Cael knowledge he has not earned. No internal power-limit breach is apparent.
    64	
    65	### Knowledge boundaries
    66	
    67	Cael marks the watcher as *probable*, not *confirmed*, before the council. The group considers alternative employers. Later conclusions are tied to observed behavior, paper changes, and repeatable counts. Seln knows he has been found because he reconstructs the door-glass test and observes the group's disciplined nonreaction; Cael never gains access to Seln's private report or thoughts. The dual viewpoint creates dramatic irony without leaking information across characters.
    68	
    69	### Reserved disclosures
    70	
    71	No reservation plan was supplied, so compliance cannot be verified. Chapter 18 is the movement's major disclosure choice: it names Seln, shows his report, confirms his service connection and Shadow practice, and reveals that he knows Cael found him. This deliberately ends the reader's “is Cael right?” uncertainty and replaces it with “what will two disciplined observers do now?” The trade works on a cold read and gives Seln welcome moral and professional complexity. It should nevertheless be checked against any external reservation plan because it is a substantial reveal, not a minor cutaway.
    72	
    73	## Repair brief
    74	
    75	### 1. Signal the chronological rewind
    76	
    77	- **Location:** End of Chapter 19, from the revised notice on day seventy-nine through the four-day recovery test; opening of Chapter 20, day seventy-seven bracket posting.
    78	- **Observed issue:** Chapter 20 moves backward at least two days—and effectively behind the closing recovery sequence—without a transition indicating that the bracket material began earlier.
    79	- **Effect on the reader:** A cold reader may stop to reconstruct dates or assume the chapters are misordered, interrupting momentum just before the movement's strongest action chapter.
    80	- **Proposed scope:** Add one short orienting phrase or sentence at the start of Chapter 20 establishing that the bracket posting occurred two days before Seln corrected the floor issue. Preserve the present chapter order if the thematic grouping is intentional; no scene restructuring is required.
    81	- **Strength to preserve:** The immediate image of Cael arriving early not to read the brackets but to watch other people read them.
    82	
    83	### 2. Trim only duplicated inference, not investigative steps
    84	
    85	- **Location:** Chapter 16 after “*He needs a crowd*”; Chapter 18 around the repeated explanations of the continuous declaration, attention toll, and late-day fatigue.
    86	- **Observed issue:** Several conclusions are delivered three times in close succession: once through the observed scene, once through Cael's explanatory thought, and once through a binder entry. The repetition is most noticeable after the evidence has already landed cleanly.
    87	- **Effect on the reader:** The methodical voice remains engaging, but younger or pace-sensitive readers may feel the story pause to prove a point they already understood.
    88	- **Proposed scope:** Make a line-level compression pass limited to restated interpretation—roughly one explanatory beat where scene, thought, and log say the same thing. Keep every failed method, test, counterexample, binder artifact, and the full Seln viewpoint. Do not shorten the developed fights.
    89	- **Strength to preserve:** Cael's intellectual honesty and the procedural pleasure of watching an inference become a tested model.
    90	
    91	### 3. Orient the measurement vocabulary once
    92	
    93	- **Location:** Chapter 20, the seeding explanation around Lira's “*22. Lira. Wind. Cu 2. (transfer)*”; Chapter 21, Bracken's explanation of standings as evidence for squad selection.
    94	- **Observed issue:** Formal Path tier/rank, ladder-book position, seasonal bracket seed, crown holder, and future tournament-squad selection are all distinct measures, but they arrive in a dense cluster.
    95	- **Effect on the reader:** A cold reader can infer the system, yet may need to reread before understanding exactly why Lira can be Copper Rank Two, seeded twenty-second, climb toward a Copper crown, and still remain Copper afterward.
    96	- **Proposed scope:** Add or sharpen one plain orienting sentence distinguishing permanent/formal classification from seasonal standing. Let the later arithmetic and examples do the rest; avoid an added exposition block.
    97	- **Strength to preserve:** The thematic payoff that every instrument can be honestly administered and still measure the wrong thing.

