    82	
    83	The Crown yard ran practice on the following afternoon, the sixty-ninth day, and Cael went down to the lower rail on the east side early, which was where he always went, and stood with his back to the upper tiers.
    84	
    85	He had chosen the spot a month ago for its view of the rings and nothing else. It was only in the last week that he had noticed what stood at the north end of the same rail.
    86	
    87	The yard had a lamp for evening sessions, a tall brass thing on a post at the north end of the floor, the height of two men. Its hood was polished every week by the porter, who was proud of it. By day its great curved belly gave back the whole yard in a smear of warm brown: the floor, the rings, and above them the tiers, rising in bent bands like the inside of a bowl. From where Cael stood, if he turned his head a little toward the rings, the lamp hung at the edge of his eye, and in its curve, very small and stretched sideways, sat the high west tiers.
    88	
    89	He could watch them without facing them.
    90	
    91	He had found it by accident three days before and had felt, at the time, a shameful little leap of pleasure, as if he had found a coin in a coat he had stopped wearing. Now he set his weight against the rail, folded his arms on it, and looked at the rings.
    92	
    93	The yard on a practice afternoon had its own smell, of chalk and of the oil the porter rubbed into the rail, and its own noise, which was not the roar of a ladder session but a low steady murmur, like a market an hour before closing. People came to practice to learn, or to be seen learning, or to sit in the sun on stone with a friend and argue about a fight neither of them was in. Two Iron girls behind him were quarrelling in whispers about whether a Mire practitioner could stick a Wind fighter's feet before she left the ground. A lecturer from the law range was asleep on the fourth tier with his hat over his face.
    94	
    95	The first ring had a Copper practice bout on it, a Force fourth-year against a Current girl, first to three clean touches. The second had two Stone boys doing a slow, careful drill that nobody was watching. The third was empty, waiting for its pair. The tiers were about a quarter full, which was ordinary for a practice afternoon. Notebooks were out along the second and third tiers, as they always were.
    96	
    97	In the brass, high on the west side, in a knot of five people, a small brown figure stood with something pale held against its chest.
    98	
    99	Cael watched the Force fourth-year.
