     1	# Chapter 21 — Instruments
     2	
     3	There was a first-year on the third practice ring who would not stop.
     4	
     5	It had been raining since the morning, a thin grey rain that made the chalk run and the boards slick, and the tiers were nearly empty. Cael was there because he was always there. The first-year was there because, as far as Cael could tell, she had decided the season was going to be won by whoever turned up on the days nobody else did. She was a Current practitioner, small and wiry, with her hair plastered flat to her skull, and she had fought four practice bouts that afternoon and lost three of them. Between bouts she stood under the eaves of the north stair, wringing water out of her sleeves, watching the next pair with a ferocious attention, and then she put her name on the slate again.
     6	
     7	Her fifth was against a Blade second-year a head taller than she was, and Cael watched it from the empty fourth tier with the rain running off the brim of his hood.
     8	
     9	She lost the first touch in about the time it took to blink. The Blade boy came in long and straight and she rode his line the way Current fighters were taught, going along the force instead of against it, and the boards were wet, and her back foot went out from under her on the chalk. She sat down hard. The instructor at the table said "Touch, Blade," without lifting his eyes from the register. She got up. She did not look at the table, or at the tiers, or at anything but the Blade boy's lead foot.
    10	
    11	The second touch was hers. It was not pretty. She went in low, deliberately short, on the wet board where her own foot had failed her, and when he came long and straight again she did not ride his line at all. She let it go over her shoulder and came up inside it with her palm on his breastbone, so close he could not have struck her if he had tried. The boy blinked down at the hand on his chest as if somebody had put it there while he was not looking.
    12	
    13	He took the third and the fourth, cleanly, because he was a year older and a head taller and simply better, and the bout was his three to one. She shook his hand. Then she went back under the eaves of the north stair and wrung out her sleeves again, and stood with her eyes on the next pair, and Cael saw her mouth moving as if she were saying something over to herself so as not to lose it.
    14	
    15	He did not know her name. He wrote her down by her smock and her Path and the touch she had taken off the wet board, *went short where she slipped; used the slip*, and then, because he was curious, went to look for her on the sheet.
    16	
    17	She was seventy-fourth.
    18	
    19	He did the sum on the steps, roughly, in his head, and then did not believe it. So he went down to the long reading room under the law range and did it properly, on paper, with the session calendar from the board by the theater door and a copy of Bracken's half-sheet on seeding laid beside it.
    20	
    21	The ladder's rules he already knew by heart. A challenge went up the column, four places at the most, and a win took the loser's place. So the quickest any name could climb was four places in an afternoon, every afternoon, against the highest name it was allowed to reach, without a loss.
    22	
    23	The calendar was the part nobody had put beside the rules.
    24	
    25	It gave a Copper name a bout about once in six days, when the pairings fell kindly. From the posting to the final was a little over a hundred days. Seventeen bouts at the very most, then, and the first of the seventeen was the drawn bout, which counted in the record and moved nobody on the sheet. Sixteen that could move a name. And that was for a name that was never injured, never deferred, never left standing on its afternoon without anybody to fight.
    26	
    27	He put the first-year's number in. Seventy-fourth. To reach the four names that would fight the final she needed to climb seventy places, four at a time, which was eighteen wins in a row.
    28	
    29	She had sixteen bouts.
    30	
    31	He sat and looked at it. He went up the column a line at a time to find where the arithmetic opened again, and found it at sixty-eighth. From sixty-eighth, a perfect season would just reach the final. From sixty-ninth and every line below it, no season could. Twenty-three names at the foot of the Copper sheet, a quarter of it, could not reach the crown even if they never lost a bout from the posting to the last day. They had been unable to reach it since the porter pressed the first pin into the board. Nothing had shut them out but a calendar and a rule, each of which, taken alone, was perfectly sensible.
    32	
    33	And every one of the twenty-three, so far as he could tell, was out on the rings in the rain, fighting for it.
    34	
