   151	He had read the brief from the back, as he read every brief. Whoever had signed it sat three grades over the officer's head. Then the line the reports would climb: past that man, past his superior, to somewhere the officer had perhaps never been. Between them, those two facts set the limit on how honest the rest could afford to be. The front came last. Halfway down its second page, he had the drafter marked as a thinking man.
   152	
   153	*Subject: enrollee, Halcenvane Academy, Ostrand; age fifteen; no classification of record. Greyvane: the challenge failed and the charter reading stands. Watch to date: open, funded, on rotation, without a break since the ruling.*
   154	
   155	Lower down came the reason anybody had troubled to write it.
   156	
   157	*Visible rotation has produced conduct data of high volume and negligible value. Subject modifies observable behavior in response to observation with a consistency that suggests deliberate management rather than nervousness. Recommend covered coverage.*
   158	
   159	He had read that twice. These days that was as close as the work came to pleasing him, and nobody in the room could have recognised the look on him. *Deliberate management rather than nervousness.* Somebody up there knew the difference. In all his years of passing product upward he had met perhaps three readers who could have told the two apart. Care in a watched man usually means fear. Care aimed *at* the watcher is speech without words, and the only sensible reply is silence, from a place he can't see.
   160	
   161	He knew the sentence: once, in a colder country, he had written its twin about another man. He went no further down that road.
   162	
   163	The officer had been talking about cadence: a product each month, a summary each quarter, exception reports when the matter called for them. Seln kept what would bind him and let the rest blow by.
   164	
   165	It was a sound brief. He turned that over as the officer talked. Whoever had drafted it had looked at a year of open watching, two men and a horse-change every so many miles, and seen what it had bought, which was a great deal of paper describing a boy being careful. The recommendation followed from that as plainly as a sum. You could not learn what a man did when unobserved by observing him in plain sight. Somebody had to sit in the room and never happen in it.
   166	
   167	Seln's trade consisted of nothing else. He had long since lost count of the rooms.
   168	
   169	Now he tucked the pages under his arm and inclined his head to the officer, who seemed faintly put out, and went out into a corridor that had windows all along one side, and found it was raining. He stood a moment at the nearest window and watched it run down the glass. Then he went to find the clerk who would give him his travel warrant, and was perfectly pleasant to her, and she forgot his face before he had reached the stair.
