   172	
   173	He built the man in a posting-house room in Ostrand over four evenings, with the rain on the shutters and the barge horns calling to each other down on the river.
   174	
   175	The hand came first, because in any building a hand outlasted the man who wrote it. Six were at his command, each sure enough to write half asleep. On the back of the ferry tally he tried them all and settled on the fourth, a small, upright, slightly cramped script, the kind a country copying-house teaches a boy and never tells him he could improve. It looked slow and was perfectly legible. Nobody, anywhere he had used it, had ever commented on it. That was all it was good for, and it was enough.
   176	
   177	The voice he tried on the posting-house boy who brought his supper. He set it a hundred-odd miles north of the river and let it be ten years behind the times there, so the vowels belonged to a town that no longer quite spoke that way.
   178	
   179	"North, is it?" said the boy, setting down the tray. "Been away long?"
   180	
   181	"A while," said Seln, in the stale voice, and looked at his plate.
   182	
   183	"Ah," said the boy. He asked nothing more, then or on any later evening. A man with a fresh accent got asked about home. A man with an old one got left alone, because people decided he had his reasons, and what people decided for themselves they never troubled to check.
   184	
   185	The coat he chose a grade below what the post would warrant. An assistant who dressed to his station looked like a man going somewhere, and people watched a man going somewhere to see where he went. Then there was the small failing. He had carried it from posting to posting and never known it to let him down. In his first week in the office he would get a sum wrong in his head, an easy one, in front of somebody, and let himself be put right. After that the office would know something about him, and would be proud of having found it out without help, and the shelf where a real opinion might have grown would already be occupied.
   186	
   187	On the fourth evening he wrote out his residence card on the form the mid-tier academies used. *Path: Shadow. Tier: Bronze.* He wrote both in the fourth hand. Both were so. Cleverer officers than he had burned through their covers by making up facts and then having to keep them straight.
   188	
   189	Then he packed.
   190	
   191	There were two cases. Into the larger went the man he would be: shirts, a razor, copying pens, a box of the oddments rooms are expected to hold, and four books he had chosen by their spines. The other case was a little thing, old, its corners bound in brass, its lock shut. He laid it at the bottom of the first, as he had laid it at the bottom of every case he had packed in his working life, and filled the first up round it, and shut the lid. He did not open the small one. He did not open it after, either.
   192	
   193	He went down to the common room for an hour before bed and let the Path run while he ate, because it was good to keep a tool warm.
   194	
   195	It worked, as it always did, on the room. Nobody's eye came back to the man in the grey coat in the corner a second time. The Path did not hide him; it only made him cheap to overlook, so each glance slid past and found somewhere better to rest. But a small girl at the next table, perhaps four years old, had looked straight at him before he sat down, and she went on looking, over her mother's arm, with the frank interest of the very young, all through her supper. That gaze was out of his reach. The Path worked on glances still on their way. A glance already landed was beyond it.
   196	
   197	He let her look, and her mother never once turned round.
   198	
   199	By the end of the hour there was the old flat greyness behind his eyes that came from holding a room's attention away from himself. It would be there in the morning. However long he slept, the morning kept it. The Path cost him his own watching; to keep the room from noticing him he had to keep noticing the room, without a pause, and four hours of that wore a man thin. Instruments were beyond it entirely; a pendulum, a counting-board, a ledger had no attention for anyone to steer. And it was useless, entirely and at once, against the rare kind of watcher who went looking for a hole where a man should be instead of the man. He had met two such people in his life. Both had been very good. A provincial academy seemed an unlikely place for a third.
   200	
   201	He was thirty-eight years old. Somewhere in the last few postings his skill had outlived his wanting. There was nobody likely to read his product who could have done it better, and there was nothing he wanted any more from having done it well. He set that aside as he would have set aside a subject's quirk: noted, of no known use.
   202	
   203	In the morning he crossed the bridge.
   204	
   205	---
   206	
   207	He walked the bluff for three days with a satchel of paper on his shoulder, and he never once looked at it the way a visitor looks at a place.
   208	
   209	He read it as water reads a hillside. Where would people go, and where would they stop, and from where could they be seen stopping? Three halls on the north edge, each with two sets of doors: six streams that ran together for a quarter of an hour at every change of bell and then broke apart again. The assessment wing sat behind a low wall of its own: one gate, then two doors into the building. Excellent, if you worked inside. Very poor for anyone who wanted to stand outside and watch the gate. The lecture range, four floors high, a stair at either end and every landing open along its whole length, so that a man on one landing could be seen from the far stair by anybody who happened to be looking. The quadrangles built round courts, so many windows facing in that nobody ever looked out of any of them on purpose.
   210	
   211	In the middle lay the sunk yard, ringed with stone tiers except where the faculty stand took up one whole side. Room for more than eleven hundred. The top tier gave a man the whole floor and half the ways in and out of it, which made it the best place on the bluff to watch from. Everybody on the bluff knew it, so the top tier was where eyes gathered, and a place where eyes gathered was a place where a watcher got noticed, which made it also the worst.
   212	
   213	He would use it regardless, since nothing else would serve. But carefully: never the same seat, never one tier two days together, never alone at a bench's open end where glances fall of their own accord. Always somewhere in the middle of a group of four or five, where the group's own shape did most of the work and the Path had only to smooth the edge.
   214	
   215	The satchel took him everywhere. A new assistant carries paper, and paper has business in every corridor on a hill.
   216	
   217	On the fourth morning he was in the assessment wing's passage with an armful of forms when the door of the demonstration room opened at the change of bell.
   218	
   219	Two instructors came out first, and a clerk with a folder, into the stream of people going by. Then, after a little, the boy came out alone, unhurried, carrying nothing, favouring the leading leg a little and holding his right arm close against his side the way a man holds an arm that has been asked to take something it did not care for. Behind him the magister had turned back into the room. Seln was perhaps thirty feet away. He stepped out of the stream, as a clerk does, to let it go by.
   220	
   221	He did not look at the boy.
   222	
   223	The face was the thing, as he had once tried to tell a young officer long ago who had neither listened nor lasted. People did not feel a stranger's nearness, or how often they had passed him, or how odd it was that he kept turning up. They felt his eyes. A man could pass the same stranger nine times on a stair and remember none of it, and on the tenth, if their eyes met once, all nine would come back to him at once as a pattern, and the stranger would be finished. No looking, then, for the first month. Only what came in at the edges: where the boy stood, how the passage moved round him, what the people round him did. Later there would be months of it, once Seln had become furniture; nobody minds a chair with eyes.
