   228	
   229	That suited him, and he noted it without warmth. The more people already stared at a man, the less anybody would notice one more.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	Karis's census of the residence cards had reached the staff corridor by then, and she had made Cael promise to read every card on it the next time he passed that way, since it was on his road back from the north hall and not on hers.
   234	
   235	"Count the ones you'd bet your supper on," she said. "And the ones you wouldn't. I'm up to sixty-one doors. I believe forty."
   236	
   237	Each door on the bluff had its card, eye-high in a little frame of brass, giving name, Path and tier. Greyvane would never have hung such cards; it had been built on the suspicion that papers lied about people, and you do not fix to a door a figure you suspect. Halcenvane put the number on every door because here the number ran everything, the ladder and the brackets and the rooms and the seeding, and a corridor of cards was simply the ledger with its pages hung up where everybody walked. Cael's own card read *Cael. Assay provision. Evaluation by demonstration.*, in letters squeezed smaller than any other on the corridor so that the words would fit. Lira's read *Wind. Copper.* Brom's read *Iron Skin. Copper.*, and Brom tapped it with a knuckle every night on his way in, as if to remind it he was there. Karis's read *Ember. Iron.* It was the one card she said she would have bet her supper on, and she used it to measure all the others.
   238	
   239	He had a filing to hand in on the way, so he went to the assessment wing first.
   240	
   241	It was his second floor-time allocation, and he walked it over himself, three days after the baseline, instead of leaving it for the residence clerk. He wanted to see the wing on a day when nobody was being measured in it. The demonstration room's door was shut. The passage smelled of lamp black and machine oil and the fine dry dust of the chalk trays, and on the records side every sheet he saw bore a pair of dates and a pair of initials, one for whoever had written it and one for whoever had checked, so that no paper could leave the wing with a single person's word on it. Vell had run her ledger like that from sheer temper; Halcenvane printed it into its forms.
   242	
   243	In the end office the regular clerk sat frowning over a tally. Under the window, at the copying table, sat somebody Cael had not seen before. He took Cael's sheet without a word and read it right through, which most clerks never did. He turned it over to look at the authorizations on the back, checked both sets of initials in the corner, laid it on the right heap without glancing at the heaps, and wrote it into the big ledger with the two dates side by side.
   244	
   245	"East and north halls. Four a week, supervised. Any change of hall reaches your residence board from this office, the evening before. Never from a hall. A notice from a hall is unauthorized; ignore it." He blotted the line. "That's been tried twice this term."
   246	
   247	"Thank you."
   248	
   249	The clerk at the desk looked up. "Four a week, and how many weeks to the semester? I've got to carry it forward."
   250	
   251	At the copying table the new man worked at it, his lips shaping figures. "Nineteen weeks. Seventy—" He stopped, and frowned. "Seventy-two."
   252	
   253	"Seventy-six," said the clerk, quite kindly, and wrote it.
   254	
   255	"Seventy-six," he repeated, with a faint flush, and returned his pen to its line.
   256	
   257	Cael lingered a moment and, without choosing to, ran the full gaze over him, as three years of roads had taught him to with every new face. The pen kept one pace throughout, through speaking and through silence: a calm temperament, or a working life spent copying, which a copier would come by honestly. His weight sat evenly, the habit of years on his feet at copying tables. His breath was ordinary, a little slow. He was where an assistant ought to be at that hour, holding what an assistant ought to hold. All four layers said the same, and what they said was that here was a man who had spent his life writing out other people's words, and was not very good at sums.
   258	
   259	Walking back up the passage, Cael could find no reason to think him anything else. He turned it over for a hundred steps and then let it drop. One time was an accident. What he had was a single ordinary man being ordinary once, and an Ardenmere spring had taught him, underlined, what it cost to build on nothing.
   260	
   261	Then he went on along the staff corridor, counting cards for Karis.
   262	
   263	Near the far end, on a door whose frame had stood empty all term, there was now a card.
   264	
   265	*Seln. Shadow. Bronze.*
   266	
   267	He stopped for a breath, perhaps two.
   268	
   269	Shadow was a Path he had never charted. Greyvane had not had one, and at the Ironyard he had seen two in eighteen months, both of them washouts who brawled and left their Path idle, since being hard to notice pays nothing in a ring that people have bought tickets to stare at. And Bronze was above an assistant's post. Mid-tier schools mostly filled their assistant posts with Copper, or with Iron fresh from its own course, people hanging on for a year. A Bronze man at a copying table had either had his road bend under him or chosen a quiet year for some private reason. Both were ordinary, and both could be found in any school on the continent.
   270	
   271	He put the card in Karis's count as one he would believe, and went to supper.
   272	
   273	He wrote up the day afterward in the observation notebook, by the open window, with the barge horns calling their long call and their two short ones on the river below: the two dates and two initials, the chalk dust, the cards and the census and what a corridor of cards said about the place that hung them. Last, beneath the new heading for the wing's staff, he put down the smallest entry of the day. Ardenmere had taught him that the small ones were the ones that came due last.
   274	
   275	*New TA in Gault's office. Bronze-tier, Shadow Path per the residence card. Doesn't look at me. Note: at Halcenvane, everyone looks at me. Filed.*
   276	
   277	One reading back: it was worth what it said and nothing beyond. He put out the lamp.

