   109	That was the method he ended up building, and it was the best piece of work he had done since the Greyvane archive: he stopped measuring the man and started measuring where other people's eyes went. He already had the instrument — the glance count, six weeks of it now, a baseline for how often attention landed on a body in a Halcenvane corridor. So he ran it on the corridor instead of on himself, sampling one stretch of the covered walk at a fixed hour, counting how many transiting people marked *anyone* standing at the walk's east rail.
   110	
   111	Then he compared the count on days the teaching assistant was at the east rail against days he was not.
   112	
   113	The difference was not large and it was absolutely consistent, and it was the first quantity anybody had ever put on a Shadow Path in Cael's presence: attention landed on that rail about a third less often when the man was standing at it. Not never. A third less. Which meant the Path was not hiding him — it was taxing everyone else's inclination to look, at a rate, and the rate was payable.
   114	
   115	And a rate meant a resource, and a resource meant a curve.
   116	
   117	He found the curve in the last week of the month, and finding it was the moment the whole study stopped being surveillance and started being Path theory, which was a distinction Karis had been making at him for a year and which he finally understood from the inside.
   118	
   119	The positions degraded over a day.
   120	
   121	Early sessions: the optimum, every time, without exception — the north end of the rail, the third body of a cluster, the second landing's inner side. Late sessions, after four hours or more on the floors: the positions got worse. Not bad. Worse. A body further out from the cluster's center. The stair-head instead of the rail, which saw two-thirds of a hall instead of all of it. Twice, near the end of long days, a position with a wall at his back and a clear view of one door only, which was the choice of a man who had stopped optimizing for coverage and started optimizing for not being approached.
   122	
   123	*Suppression is not free,* Cael wrote, and underlined it, because it was the sentence the whole month had been for. *It runs on something that depletes across a day and recovers overnight. Four hours looks like the shoulder of the curve. After that he stops buying good sightlines and starts buying cheap ones, which is exactly what I do with the Wind-adjacent when the hip's already paid twice.*
   124	
   125	*Every ability I have ever charted has a price. I have been assuming his didn't, because I couldn't see him paying it. He was paying it in front of me all month, in the only currency his Path can pay in: the quality of where he chooses to stand.*
   126	
   127	He wrote the limits down too, on the facing page, in the third column he had kept since Ardenmere and had never once let himself skip, because a study that recorded only what it had found was a study that would eventually claim to have found everything.
   128	
   129	*Discarded / cannot reach:*
   130	
   131	*— Range. I have no idea what the suppression's radius is. Every observation I have is of him standing still in a populated space. I have never seen him move under it at speed and I have no way to make that happen without asking for it.*
   132	
   133	*— Contact. Does it hold against touch? Against being spoken to? He is spoken to constantly in that office and it holds, so probably yes, but the office isn't looking for him and I don't know what happens against somebody who is.*
   134	
   135	*— The floor. He has never once been on a training floor in a training capacity. Bronze-tier and he does not spar, does not drill, does not so much as stretch in a public hall. That's not modesty. It's the same decision I'd make: a Path only shows what you let it be used for, and he has decided that his gets used for exactly one thing in this building. I would very much like to see him use it for something else, and I am not going to get to.*
   136	
   137	*— And the largest gap, which I'm writing plainly so I stop pretending it isn't there: everything above is inferred from where a man chooses to stand. It's all one instrument. Vell would say a rating built on one link is a rating three-quarters made up. She'd be right.*
   138	
   139	*Note, and I want this on the page in my handwriting: he is not hiding from me. He stopped being able to hide from me on day twenty-three. He is hiding from four hundred other people, all day, every day, so that the one person who can see him doesn't matter. That is a better answer to being caught than anything I would have come up with, and I have been thinking about it for two weeks.*
   140	
   141	---
   142	
   143	Lira put him on the floor twice that week and had opinions about the second one.
   144	
   145	They trained in hall one at the sixth bell on the days her ladder schedule allowed it, which by then was two days in five, and she had been fighting him at genuine intensity since the second week because the Halcenvane floors were better than Greyvane's and she had said, in the flat way she said things she'd already decided, that it would be a waste not to use them.
   146	
   147	The first fall was ordinary and his own fault. The second was the informative one.
   148	
   149	He had gone to the Wind-adjacent on the fourth exchange, which was right — she had committed forward on a diagonal and the burst was the correct answer and he had priced it before reaching for it, the way the log had required for a year and a half. The hips dropped. The floor gave him the half-inch it kept in reserve. The held half-breath started and his weight simply stopped being where her read said it was, hollow-boned for the length of it, a half-body-width of displacement sideways that his legs had no business producing — and her committed motion went past him into air.
   150	
   151	Then the landing beat.
   152	
   153	That was the price, and it was not negotiable and never had been: the borrowed lightness returning to ordinary mass all at once, a jolt up through both heels, and half a breath of pure locked commitment in which he could not change direction if the building came down. He had used that half-breath correctly four hundred times. He used it wrong this time, because he was a beat late deciding what to do with it, because the surface read had come in late again, because the surface read had been coming in late for three weeks and he knew exactly why.
   154	
   155	Lira hit him with her shoulder and put him on the boards, and stood over him with her hands on her knees, breathing hard, entirely unapologetic.
   156	
   157	"That's twice," she said.
   158	
   159	"I know."
   160	
   161	"Your read's slow."
   162	
   163	"I know that too."
   164	
   165	"Then say the rest of it out loud instead of writing it in the book where I have to wait for it."
   166	
   167	He got his elbows under him. The boards at Halcenvane were sprung wood over stone, which was a great deal kinder than the Ironyard's foundry floor and had still knocked most of the air out of him. "The surface read runs on the same attention the counting runs on. I've been spending it on corridors for a month. So it's arriving a beat late, and a beat late is the difference between using the landing and eating your shoulder."
   168	
   169	"Right." She put a hand down and he took it. "So what's it worth?"
   170	
   171	"The counting?"
   172	
   173	"The counting."
   174	
   175	He thought about it properly, which she waited for, because she always waited for it.
   176	
   177	"A month ago the Compact could see everything I do and I couldn't see any of it," he said. "Now I know where their man stands, what his Path costs him, when in the day he gets tired, and what he can't do at all. That's the best trade I've ever made in my life." He rolled his shoulder and found it working. "And it cost me a beat off the Iron-adjacent for a month, which cost me two falls to you, which cost me a bruise. So: yes. But I'm writing the bruise down."
   178	
   179	Lira looked at him for a second and then, unexpectedly, laughed — one short, real one.
   180	
   181	"You're the only person I've ever met," she said, "who'd file a bruise."
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	The schedule came back on a Fifth-day, in the ordinary way, through the residence board.
   186	
   187	Assessment-office paper, assessment-office hand, two dates in the corner and two sets of initials, the whole apparatus doing exactly what it did. *Supervised training allocation, revised issue.* And on the third line, where the previous issue had said *hall one*, this one said *hall three*.
   188	
   189	Cael stood at the board and read it twice, and something in his chest did a slow, cold, delighted turn.
   190	
   191	He had noticed the error on the Second-day. Three days ago. The previous issue's third line had listed his Sixth-day slot in hall one, and the master ledger — which he had watched being written, standing at the copying table, in the first week — held hall three, and the discrepancy was a transposition of the kind that a copying office running four hundred enrollees' paperwork produced perhaps twice a week and corrected when somebody complained.
   192	
   193	He had not complained.
   194	
   195	He had thought about it for most of a morning and had then made a deliberate decision and written it down, because decisions unwritten had a way of becoming instincts he couldn't audit later: he would leave the error in place and see whether it corrected itself, and if it did, he would know who was reading his paperwork closely enough to care that it was wrong.
   196	
   197	Because that was the thing about the error. It cost Cael almost nothing — he'd have arrived at hall one, been redirected, walked six minutes, lost a quarter of a session. Annoying. Trivial.
   198	
   199	It cost a man building a positional map of Cael's week a great deal more than that. An observer working from the filed schedule would have been standing at the hall one gallery on Sixth-day, four bodies in from the stair, at a rail overlooking a floor his subject was never going to walk onto. A whole session of coverage, gone, on a document error nobody had reported.
   200	
   201	So the error had been fixed. Quietly. Unrequested. Three days after Cael had declined to mention it, by an office he had said nothing to, on a form that carried two dates and two sets of initials and no note whatsoever.
   202	
   203	He stood at the residence board in the Fifth-day traffic with four hundred people going past behind him and understood, with total clarity, several things at once.
   204	
   205	The man knew he had left it. Of course he knew — leaving an error uncorrected for three days was itself an observable behavior, and Cael had been observed for three days by somebody with a professional reason to notice paperwork.
   206	
   207	The man had fixed it anyway. Which was a message, and the message was not *I know*. The message was *I know, and I am going to keep doing the work properly, and here is my paperwork, correct, on time, initialed twice.*
   208	
   209	And Cael had built the test in the first place, which meant Cael had said something first, and the correction was an answer, and neither of them had spoken a word to the other since a filing date four weeks ago.
   210	
   211	He took the sheet off the board, folded it, and put it in his coat, and went to his session.
   212	
   213	That night he wrote one line under the day's entry, and then sat looking at it for a while before he closed the book.
   214	
   215	*He fixed it. Sixth-day, hall three. Nobody asked him to.*
   216	
   217	*We're playing now. Neither of us has said so. I don't think either of us is going to.*

