    14	
    15	The brass mark meant defending champion. Fiske had worn it for two seasons.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	The ladder's mechanics were simpler than the politics that hung off them, which Cael had come to expect of institutions generally.
    20	
    21	Bouts ran on scheduled session afternoons, four to a session, on the Crown yard floor under two faculty — one adjudicating, one on safety. A challenge went up the column, never down; you could call anyone within four places above you and nobody below. A win moved you into the loser's place and pushed everyone between down a step. A loss cost nothing directly, which had surprised him until he'd understood the design: the standings punished inactivity instead, because a name that went four sessions without a bout slid a place on its own. The standing was not the tier — the tier was what the registry said you were, fixed until a registry chose to say otherwise, and the standing was only where you sat inside the cage the tier had put you in. The system's whole engine was the requirement to keep fighting. Sit still and the ladder ate you.
    22	
    23	At the season's end the top four in each bracket ran a witnessed final for the crown, and the crown itself was a real object — Cael had seen the Copper one in the case outside Withrow's records hall, a circlet of hammered copper the width of two fingers, unremarkable as metalwork and clearly, obviously, a thing people wanted.
    24	
    25	And underneath all of it, the fact that made the whole yard hum: standings fed team consideration, and next year the qualifying season for the Continental Academy Tournament opened, and Halcenvane intended to contest it. Withrow had said so, publicly, in her second address of the term, in the plain register of a woman who did not consider ambition something to apologize for. Every enrollee in the covered walk that morning knew that a season's standing was going to be read next year by people selecting a team, and knew that being selected for a continental qualifying squad was the single largest career event available to any of them.
    26	
