    64	
    65	This was the other one. He'd heard it exactly twice in his life, both times in the Ironyard, both times when something had happened on the floor that the room's whole understanding of what it was watching could not absorb. It was the sound a crowd makes when it has just watched a category fail — when ninety-one names on a board have been sorted into a column by a system, and the system's answer has been publicly and unarguably wrong about one of them, and everybody in the yard understands this without any of them being able to say what they understand.
    66	
