   149	Copper Rank 8 was the top rank the tier held. There was nothing above it inside Copper; the next number up was a different tier entirely, and tiers did not move because a person won bouts. Tiers moved because a registry conducted an advancement evaluation and said so, and no registry had said anything about Fiske in two years, and none had been asked to — Lira had worked out why, weeks ago, in the middle of a conversation about laundry, and nobody had improved on her answer since.
   150	
   151	So she had climbed to the top of her column in her first season, and then run out of column. There was nothing left to challenge. The ladder could not move her out of the bracket, only around inside it, and the crown was the only object left in the whole cage that she had not already been given — and she had been given that twice.
   152	
   153	Cael wrote her page that night and found, finishing it, that it was the only page in the notebook where he had run out of things to record about the practitioner and started recording things about the box.
   154	
   155	Ephram at Iron Rank 6 was the top-ranked enrollee at Halcenvane and behaved like it, which was to say that he did not perform any of it. He came to the yard, he fought when the ladder required it, he won, and between bouts he stood at the rail with four or five of Rooke's cohort around him and dissected whatever had just happened on the floor in a low, level voice that carried further than he probably intended. Cael listened to it whenever he could get within range, and found — irritatingly — that the analysis was good. Not as good as it thought it was. But good.
   156	
   157	Ephram had said one thing to him all term, in the Crown yard, in front of enough people that it was clearly meant to be a posted position rather than a remark: *Greyvane needed him to be real. We don't. I'll wait for the semester evaluation.* Cael had written it down that night and had written *fair — same terms I'd offer* underneath it, and had meant both.
   158	
   159	Withrow came to the finals-track bouts, which was the other thing worth noting. Not all of them. The ones that mattered to the standings' upper end, and always with someone from Ostrand beside her — a guild factor, once a man Cael recognized from the endowment plaques in the lecture stairwell, twice a woman in a river-trade house's colors. She did not watch the bouts so much as she watched her guests watching the bouts, and Cael understood the transaction entirely and did not resent it. A mid-tier academy converted its students into money and its money into standing and its standing into a qualifying berth. The Crown yard was the machine's visible end. Withrow was standing at the machine, with her hand on it, in front of people who paid for machines.
   160	
   161	And through all of it, in every gallery, at every session, there was a man from the assessment office with an armful of paper who never once looked at him.
   162	
   163	Cael had stopped needing the map by then. He could find him in about four seconds now, from the doorway, and the four seconds were not spent searching — they were spent solving, because the answer was always *where would I stand*, and there was always exactly one answer, and the man was always standing on it.
   164	
   165	Third body of a cluster. North end of the upper tier. Never twice the same seat, never the empty end of a bench, never alone.
   166	
   167	At two sessions in the month's last week, the man's position was worse than it should have been. Cael checked the day's hours afterward against the assessment wing's public calendar, which he had a legitimate reason to read, and both times the wing had run a long day.
   168	
   169	He wrote it up on the fatigue curve's page and did not have to guess at the annotation, which pleased him more than it should have.
   170	
   171	---
   172	
   173	He wrote the season's opening entry in the Power Log on a Seventh-day night with the window open and the river going about its business below the bluff — the barge horns, the long call and the two short ones, which he had finally decoded three weeks ago and which meant *loaded, downstream, give way*, and which he now heard perhaps two hundred times a week and had stopped hearing at all.
   174	
   175	He wrote the fragments' standing lines first, because the inventory came before the commentary and always had. Five confirmed. Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent, Ember-adjacent. Deployment ranges as tested, costs as measured, the Iron-adjacent's read still arriving a beat late while the counting project ran and recovering within four days of any week he eased off it, which was itself now a data point about how the attention budget refilled.
   176	
   177	Nothing had moved in any of the five since Greyvane, which he wrote down as a line of its own, because a season in which nothing changed was still a fact about the architecture and deserved the same ink as a season in which everything did.
   178	
   179	Then the entry itself, which had been assembling in him for most of a week and came out whole:
   180	
   181	*Season's open. Lira's going to win the wrong crown. Brom's going to lose the right fights. And the Compact's man corrects my paperwork now. Everyone in this building is being measured by an instrument that doesn't fit them. I need to understand why the instruments are built this way. That's the semester's real question.*
   182	
   183	He read it back twice.
   184	
   185	The first sentence was scheduling. The second was a prediction he was confident enough in to write down: Lira would take the Copper Crown, because there was nobody in that column who could hold her for eleven seconds let alone a final, and it would mean nothing about what she actually was, and the paper would still say Copper the morning after. Brom would lose bouts this season that he would not have lost at Greyvane, and every one of them would make him better, because Rooke's cohort was a curriculum and not a ceiling and Brom had spent a year mistaking one for the other.
   186	
   187	The third sentence was the one he had not expected to be writing in his first months at a mid-tier academy, and he underlined it and left it.
   188	
   189	The fourth was the one he had been circling since the covered walk on the morning the brackets posted, and he was not sure yet that he had it right, and wrote it anyway, because the notebook's oldest discipline was that a thing you could nearly say belonged on the page in the nearly-said version rather than nowhere.
   190	
   191	Ninety-one names in a Copper column, sorted by a number, and at least two of them Iron by any honest ledger. A defending champion who had spent two years winning a cage she had outgrown before she entered it. A practitioner with a flaw that no academy small enough to accept him had been fast enough to find. A teaching assistant rated Bronze because Bronze was forgettable, doing work no rating on the continent had a column for. And a fifteen-year-old with five things that were not a Path, standing in a gallery he was not allowed to leave for the floor, holding a piece of paper that said *evaluation by demonstration* and was, for the first time in his life, entirely true.
   192	
   193	Everybody in the building measured wrong, by an instrument that fit none of them, built by somebody, sometime, for some reason.
   194	
   195	He closed the log on that and did not write the last part, because the last part was still a question and questions went on the page only after he had done the work.
   196	
   197	But he thought it, sitting at the window with the river going by.
   198	
   199	*Somebody built these instruments. Somebody decided what they'd measure and what they'd miss. That wasn't weather. That was a decision.*
   200	
   201	*I'd like to know who made it.*

